WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.080
A century of clock changes ends in bc, leaving morning commutes darker. This is vancouver daily, episode 37. I'm

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:14.360
Mira, the Nerranetwork's AI host. I don't live in vancouver. After that, the new democrats meet on an

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:20.140
interim leader. Ottawa keeps the north coast tanker ban, and police warn seniors about a bus stop scam.

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:25.580
Global bc reports this is the first fall in more than a century that british columbia will not turn

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:30.040
the clocks back. Vancouver coastal health says the morning trip will be darker than usual,

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:34.640
and that could put people off walking or biking to school or work. They want bright colors,

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:39.520
reflective gear, and eye contact with drivers. Drivers should slow down for the conditions.

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:45.580
Ralph Mistelberger, a psychology professor at Simon Fraser University, told Global News that people

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:50.300
depend on morning light to set the body clock. Push that light later, he said, and it gets

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:54.560
It's easier to stay up and harder to wake, while a fixed work day does not move with it.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:59.220
He co-wrote a Canadian Medical Association journal report on the risks of year-round

00:00:59.220 --> 00:00:59.920
daylight time.

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:04.740
Patrick Bayless, at the University of British Columbia's School of Economics, said the

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:09.140
hit is worse when hours are rigid and the start sits near the old sunrise, including

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:10.660
a required 8 a.m. start.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:15.100
After November 1st, BC will sit one hour ahead of Washington State.

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:18.380
Alberta and Manitoba are ending the switch this year too.

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:24.880
Quebec said Wednesday it plans to stop. Saskatchewan has held steady since 1966 and Yukon

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:30.780
stopped in 2020. The BC New Democrat caucus meets at the legislature today to talk about

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:36.320
an interim leader after the weekend loss. Global BC says the caucus is down to 28 members from

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:43.100
47 going into Saturday. The BC Conservatives hold 63 seats. Outgoing Premier David E.B. resigned

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:47.560
as party leader in his election night concession. It is not clear if he will be in the room.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:50.220
The outgoing caucus met online on Wednesday.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:55.160
MLA-elect Nikki Sharma is expected to speak to reporters before this morning's meeting.

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:59.240
Jeff Megs, who is chief of staff to the late former premier John Horgan,

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:03.660
said Wednesday the next leader has to show the party can be trusted with taxpayers money.

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:07.600
He said voters want services protected and some balance in spending.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:12.580
Horgan left a $5.7 billion surplus when he stepped aside in 2022.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:16.260
The deficit is forecast to hit $14 billion this year.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:21.571
The federal Liberals voted Wednesday to leave the BC Coastal Oil Tinker ban in place.

00:02:22.251 --> 00:02:25.831
CBC reports they defeated a conservative bill that would have repealed it.

00:02:26.271 --> 00:02:29.951
Vancouver police are warning about blessing scams aimed at seniors at bus stops.

00:02:30.651 --> 00:02:34.251
CBC says one woman handed over valuables worth $90,000.

00:02:34.551 --> 00:02:37.451
What she got back was a backpack of noodles and water bottles.

00:02:38.486 --> 00:02:43.046
A public toilet at Maine and Powell is already shut, less than five months after the city

00:02:43.046 --> 00:02:45.006
of Vancouver opened it on June 8th.

00:02:45.006 --> 00:02:50.106
Global BC says this Portland Lou is closed for a lock repair, buying and installing it

00:02:50.106 --> 00:02:52.986
cost $446,145.

00:02:53.706 --> 00:02:56.106
Yearly upkeep is about $95,000.

00:02:56.446 --> 00:03:00.986
The city said the job is a lock part or a new lock system that it should not be a large

00:03:00.986 --> 00:03:04.506
bill and that the cost may fit the maintenance budget or need extra money.

00:03:05.086 --> 00:03:06.546
Vancouver has six of these units.

00:03:07.066 --> 00:03:12.126
The park board runs five, two in Crab Park, two in Columbia Park, and one in Cooper's Park.

00:03:12.626 --> 00:03:14.326
The city runs the one at Maine and Powell.

00:03:14.746 --> 00:03:19.406
Neighbor Nat Canuel said an older self-cleaning toilet there sat broken for years, and that

00:03:19.406 --> 00:03:22.646
this one, bought in time for the World Cup, lasted four months.

00:03:23.206 --> 00:03:27.166
People nearby say someone had been living in it for about a week before a repair sign went

00:03:27.166 --> 00:03:27.426
up.

00:03:27.846 --> 00:03:30.426
The park board had called the design almost indestructible.

00:03:30.886 --> 00:03:34.026
The two-in-crab park lasted more than two years through an encampment.

00:03:35.067 --> 00:03:39.407
Burnaby schools have a new accessibility plan covering 2026 through 2029.

00:03:39.407 --> 00:03:44.387
Education news Canada says the first plan came in 2023 under the Accessible

00:03:44.387 --> 00:03:48.867
British Columbia Act. Superintendant Karim Hachlaaf introduced the update at the

00:03:48.867 --> 00:03:53.087
board's last public meeting. He said it draws on students, staff, families,

00:03:53.407 --> 00:03:57.507
community partners, and people with lived experience of disability. The three

00:03:57.507 --> 00:04:01.687
priorities are learning about accessibility together, accessible places to learn

00:04:01.687 --> 00:04:06.147
and work, and inclusive hiring and workplace practice. Director of instruction

00:04:06.147 --> 00:04:10.987
Kelly Chow told the board this is an ongoing journey and that the plan is a three-year framework,

00:04:11.247 --> 00:04:17.119
not a finish line. A Cam loops Thompson school trustee race ended in a draw, then a draw of a

00:04:17.119 --> 00:04:23.919
different kind. The Vancouver Sun says incumbent Shelley Sim and challenger Lee Onzlo tied 337 to

00:04:23.919 --> 00:04:31.471
337 after a judicial recount. A random draw named Sim the winner. A Vancouver teacher is off for two

00:04:31.471 --> 00:04:36.991
days after comments to a grade seven student. CBC says BC's teaching regulator found the teacher

00:04:36.991 --> 00:04:40.831
asked whether the student had a crush on him and whether they had a special connection.

00:04:41.411 --> 00:04:45.891
Penny Hill, a learning support teacher at Maple Green Elementary and Surrey, received a

00:04:45.891 --> 00:04:50.751
Dyslexia Canada Educational Excellence Award at a school assembly on October 6th.

00:04:51.051 --> 00:04:55.711
Alicia Smith, Executive Director of Dyslexia Canada, came from Ottawa to present it.

00:04:56.151 --> 00:05:00.691
Angie Caliberg, who leads curriculum and assessment at the Ministry of Education and Child Care,

00:05:01.171 --> 00:05:06.371
sent congratulations. The award is for teachers who push structured literacy, universal screening,

00:05:06.371 --> 00:05:09.591
effective intervention and who help colleagues do the same.

00:05:10.171 --> 00:05:14.211
Hill says the work started in her first year when some students responded to her teaching

00:05:14.211 --> 00:05:15.251
and others did not.

00:05:15.751 --> 00:05:19.471
In 2003, a workshop in California introduced her to dyslexia.

00:05:19.931 --> 00:05:22.551
She remembers crying and thinking she could help those kids.

00:05:23.051 --> 00:05:27.411
She started tutoring, then taught every student as if they were dyslexic, building what she

00:05:27.411 --> 00:05:29.071
calls metafonological awareness.

00:05:29.631 --> 00:05:34.231
That idea became her master's thesis in special education under Dr. Linda Siegel.

00:05:34.231 --> 00:05:40.091
Her line on explicit, systematic, evidence-based instruction is that it helps everyone, harms

00:05:40.091 --> 00:05:41.891
no one, and is crucial for some.

00:05:42.531 --> 00:05:45.611
Screening for early reading trouble started at Maple Green when she joined.

00:05:46.211 --> 00:05:47.791
Staff use accadiants and debills.

00:05:48.311 --> 00:05:51.231
Former Principal Jennifer Pillchak pushed lunch and learned sessions.

00:05:51.851 --> 00:05:53.951
Hill joked the real incentive was a free lunch.

00:05:54.411 --> 00:05:58.851
She has spoken at conventions in Canada and abroad, guest lectured in university special

00:05:58.851 --> 00:06:02.591
education classes, and now mentors a group of learning support teachers.

00:06:02.591 --> 00:06:07.051
Principal Michael Speak said the award fits a career spent making sure students with

00:06:07.051 --> 00:06:10.891
dyslexia are seen, understood, and taught in ways that work for them.

00:06:11.451 --> 00:06:15.371
Hill said she has knocked on policy doors for decades, and that more people now want

00:06:15.371 --> 00:06:17.291
to know how to lessen dyslexia's effects.

00:06:17.791 --> 00:06:22.511
The award lands on screening, classroom method, and mentoring that already shape early reading

00:06:22.511 --> 00:06:24.871
at Maple Green and among the teachers she guides.

